\\begin{aligned}34+18 & ≡52 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,48) \\\\  & ≡48+4 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,48) \\\\  & ≡4 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,48) \\\\  & ≢0 & \\,(\\text{mod}\\,48)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude the following:\n\nThe integer $34$ $\\text{is not}$ the additive inverse of $18$ modulo $48$ since $34+18≢0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,48).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415389 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415389 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213351" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415389 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415389 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $19,$ the additive inverse of $-15$ modulo $19$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415389 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0724cde5-d299-58a6-b45c-db140b3f5272" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415389 #edb/ref 1074 "The additive inverse of $-15$ modulo $19$ is the integer $0\\le x<19$ such that\n\n$$\nx+(-15)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,19).\n$$\n\nNotice that $15+(-15)≡0,$ so $x≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,19),$ which is already in the required form. \n\nTherefore, the additive inverse of the element $-15$ modulo $19$ is $15.$\n\n**Note:** In general, the additive inverse of $a$ modulo $n$ is simply the residue of $-a$ modulo $n,$ i.e. $-a\\,\\text{mod}\\,n.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415390 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186415367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415390 #edb/ref 1069 "q-213350" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415390 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415390 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as a nonnegative integer smaller than $14,$ the additive inverse of $-10$ modulo $14$ is{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415390 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "087515b4-1dee-5eec-87da-466a8e426d85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415390 #edb/ref 1074 "The additive inverse of $-10$ modulo $14$ is the integer $0\\le x<14$ such that\n\n$$\nx+(-10)≡0\\,(\\text{mod}\\,14).\n$$\n\nNotic